@startuml check-levels
' The four levels of verification of Yeah, from the cheapest to the strongest,
' and the boundary of what they prove.
'
' A unit test proves a transition of the kernel: a pure function, a
' validation, a projection.  A canary proves an invariant that a claim names,
' and every canary carries a control that can make the check fail.  An
' integration test proves that the parts of the kernel talk to each other: a
' real gptel, a real process, a real protocol, a restart and a recovery.  A
' live test proves that a real provider answers.
'
' The levels are ordered by the strength of the proof, and the boundary is on
' the picture on purpose: no level proves the product.  A green module is not
' a working product, and the top of the ladder is still a kernel in a
' prototype.
'
' The places of the levels are the places of the tree: the directories of the
' tests and the gate that runs them.

start
:the gate of the project runs the checks of the tree
and binds the evidence to the claims
scripts/gate;

:unit
(pure transitions, validation, projections, helpers)
tests/unit;

:contract / canary
(the failure classes and the invariants named by claims,
each with a control that can make the check fail)
tests/checks;

:integration
(real gptel, process and protocol round trips,
restart, recovery, the whole coding scenario)
tests/checks and tests/live;

:live / evaluation
(pinned real providers, comparative tasks against
opencode and pi, Emacs-native tasks)
tests/live;

:the boundary of the proof
(every level proves its own subject,
a green module is not a working product);

note right
  This picture does not show the work the
  levels ran on.  Still open at the top of
  the ladder: no real ACP editor has
  finished a task, the clean bootstrap does
  not run in CI, and the bridge is off
  until the operator enables it.
end note
stop
@enduml
